  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAfter scattered rain showers come to an end this evening across\nmost of eastern New York and western New England, patchy fog can\ndevelop this evening into tomorrow morning as clouds decrease\nand moisture at the surface supports patchy dense fog\ndevelopment. Across the Mid-Hudson Valley, Taconics, Berkshires,\nLitchfield Hills, and southern Vermont, the latest forecast \ncontinues to support low to medium (25 to 50 percent) chances \nfor scattered light to moderate rain showers through the day\ntomorrow as a weather system stalls to our east and periods of\nbeneficial rain continues. Rainfall amounts can range between\n0.25 inches and 0.75 inches ending 7 AM Saturday. Due to the\nrecent extended long period of dry conditions and steadiness of\nrainfall (light to moderate), we are not expecting any flooding\nto occur. Scattered rain showers come to a brief end late Friday\nnight and early Saturday morning before the next weather\nsystem arrives. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA weather system is forecasted to arrive across eastern New York\nand western New England Saturday bringing additional beneficial\nrainfall. The latest forecast continues to support medium to\nhigh (greater than 50 percent) chances of scattered to\nwidespread rain showers Saturday morning. Rain showers should\nmove from west to east across eastern New York and western New \nEngland and continue through Sunday morning. Rainfall amounts \nrange between 0.25 and 0.75 inches. As the nature of the\nrainfall will be light to moderate at times, no flooding impacts\nare expected. \n\nTemperatures for the middle of November trend towards near\nnormal as we head into a favorable weather pattern for daily low\nchances of precipitation and cooler morning low temperatures.\nMonday through Wednesday mornings next week could range in the\n20s and 30s across eastern New York and western New England.\nHigh temperatures could range in the 40s and 50s, which is near\nnormal for this time of year across the high terrain and valley\nlocations.",
